    {
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "Thanks for the fix — this is correct. The truncation was my misreading in #8627: the CUDA kernel goes through `conversion::to<int32_t>` (`__float2int_rn`, round half to even), not a C-style cast, so this now matches both the kernel and the `torch.round` reference in `test_quantize.py`.\r\n\r\nVerified on Ascend 910B4 (CANN 8.5.1, torch_npu 2.10.0.post7):\r\n\r\n  | Check | trunc (before) | round (this PR) |\r\n  | --- | --- | --- |\r\n  | qgZ int4 reconstruction, mean err (on device) | 0.1198 | 0.0506 |\r\n  | qgZ int4 reconstruction, max err (on device) | 0.3511 | 0.2349 |\r\n  | 2-node × 2-device qgZ sum / exact sum (CPU sim) | 0.845 | 0.995 |\r\n  | This test (int4 + int8) | — | 2/2 pass |\r\n  | Per-group numpy-oracle round-trip suite (on device) | — | all pass |\r\n\r\nOne small suggestion on the test: `test_quantize.py` already covers this contract across both hardware backends — `QuantizerBuilder().load()` resolves to `NPUQuantizer` on NPU (the path your 60/60 run exercised) and to the CUDA kernel on CUDA, both validated against the same `torch.round` reference.\r\n\r\nIt might be cleaner to reuse it directly instead of adding a separate NPU-specific file, so the reference logic stays consolidated in one place. The int4 `swizzle_quant` coverage is great new coverage and worth keeping — and since it’s pure torch as well, it can fit into the existing test file without needing an NPU-specific variant.",
